Below is a step-by-step AWS VPC lab guide that you can use for teaching or hands-on practice.

The architecture will contain:

1 VPC
1 Internet Gateway
2 subnets:
App Subnet — public
DB Subnet — private
1 custom route table for the App subnet
0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway
App subnet associated with the custom route table
1 EC2 instance launched in the App subnet
Security Group allowing SSH and HTTP

Important: A subnet becomes effectively public when its route table has a route to an Internet Gateway and the instance has a public IPv4 address. AWS also notes that the security group must permit the required traffic.

AWS VPC Lab: App and DB Subnets with EC2
1. Architecture

The final architecture will look like:

                         INTERNET
                             |
                             |
                    +----------------+
                    | Internet       |
                    | Gateway        |
                    +----------------+
                             |
                             |
                    +-------------------+
                    |      VPC          |
                    |   10.0.0.0/16     |
                    |                   |
                    |  +-------------+  |
                    |  | App Subnet   |  |
                    |  | 10.0.1.0/24  |  |
                    |  |              |  |
                    |  | EC2 Instance |  |
                    |  | Public IP    |  |
                    |  +-------------+  |
                    |        |          |
                    |        |          |
                    |  App Route Table  |
                    |  0.0.0.0/0 -> IGW |
                    |                   |
                    |  +-------------+  |
                    |  | DB Subnet   |  |
                    |  | 10.0.2.0/24 |  |
                    |  |             |  |
                    |  | Database    |  |
                    |  +-------------+  |
                    |                   |
                    +-------------------+

AWS route tables determine where traffic from a subnet is directed, and a subnet can be explicitly associated with a custom route table.

2. IP Address Design

We will use the following CIDR ranges:

Resource	CIDR
VPC	10.0.0.0/16
App Subnet	10.0.1.0/24
DB Subnet	10.0.2.0/24

The important point is that the subnet ranges must be inside the VPC CIDR and must not overlap.

Address layout
VPC
10.0.0.0/16
│
├── App Subnet
│   10.0.1.0/24
│
└── DB Subnet
    10.0.2.0/24
