AWS specifically documents adding 0.0.0.0/0 to the subnet route table with the Internet Gateway as the target to provide IPv4 internet access.

11. Step 9 — Associate App Subnet with Route Table

This is a very important step.

Select:

app-public-rt

Go to:

Subnet associations
→ Edit subnet associations

Select:

app-subnet

Click:

Save associations

Now:

app-subnet
     |
     v
app-public-rt
     |
     v
0.0.0.0/0
     |
     v
Internet Gateway

A subnet can have only one route-table association at a time, while a route table can be associated with multiple subnets.

12. Step 10 — What About the DB Subnet?

For this lab, do not associate the DB subnet with the public route table.

The DB subnet will continue using the VPC's main route table unless you create and associate a separate private route table.

Therefore:

App Subnet
10.0.1.0/24
      |
      v
app-public-rt
      |
      +---- 0.0.0.0/0 → IGW


DB Subnet
10.0.2.0/24
      |
      v
Main Route Table
      |
      +---- Local VPC traffic

This is a basic public/private subnet design.

Important: If later you want resources in the DB subnet to initiate outbound internet connections for updates, you would normally introduce a NAT Gateway rather than attaching the DB subnet directly to an Internet Gateway. AWS documents NAT as the alternative when private-subnet resources need internet access without public IP addresses.

13. Step 11 — Enable Public IPv4 for App Subnet

Because we want to launch a public EC2 instance, the instance needs a public IPv4 address.

Go to:

VPC
→ Subnets
→ app-subnet
→ Actions
→ Edit subnet settings

Enable:

Enable auto-assign public IPv4 address

Save.

Alternatively, you can explicitly select public IP assignment while launching the EC2 instance.

AWS notes that resources using an Internet Gateway need a public IPv4/IPv6 address for internet connectivity.

